6. Data Preparation

The employee dataset was prepared before performing the analysis.

The preparation process included:

Reviewing the dataset structure
Checking data types
Checking for missing values
Checking for duplicate records
Reviewing categorical values
Preparing the dataset for analysis
Creating a structured dataset for Power BI

The prepared dataset was then used across Excel, SQL, Python, and Power BI.

7. Excel Analysis

Excel was used for initial data exploration and analysis.

The analysis included:

Employee data exploration
Filtering and sorting
Pivot tables
Department analysis
Gender analysis
Attrition analysis
Salary analysis
Employee tenure analysis
Charts and summary tables

Excel provided an initial understanding of workforce patterns before performing deeper analysis using SQL and Python.

8. SQL Analysis

SQL was used to perform structured analysis on the HR dataset.

The analysis focused on:

Total employee count
Attrition count
Attrition rate
Department-wise employee distribution
Department-wise attrition
Gender distribution
Salary analysis
Years-at-company analysis
Job role analysis

SQL helped transform raw employee records into business-focused metrics that could be used for HR decision-making.

9. Python Analysis

Python was used for data processing and exploratory data analysis.

Libraries used:

Pandas
NumPy
Matplotlib
Seaborn

Python was used to:

Load and inspect the dataset
Perform data manipulation
Group and aggregate employee data
Analyze workforce patterns
Analyze attrition
Create visualizations
Validate analytical findings
10. Power BI Dashboard

Power BI was used to create the final interactive HR Analytics Dashboard.

The dashboard provides a centralized view of important HR metrics and allows users to interact with the data through filters, charts, KPIs, and drill-through functionality.

Key KPIs
Total Employees
Attrition Rate
Average Salary
Department-wise Attrition
Gender Distribution
Years at Company
